.he "disk3 (4)                   10/15/26                    disk3 (4)"
.fo "                               -#-"
.in 5
.rm 65
.bd
DEVICE
.in +5
.br
.bd
disk3
- a hard disk on a CompuPro DISK 3 controller
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
FUNCTION
.in +5
.br
The
.bd
disk3
device reads and writes a hard disk attached to a CompuPro DISK 3
controller.  The board drives ST-506 disks, and it is the intelligent
one of the two CompuPro hard disk controllers: an 8085 and its EPROM
firmware run the drive, so the host's only real time work is the rate
the data moves at.  There is no step pulse anywhere in this driver.  A
seek is a command the board runs by itself, and a read or a write
addresses an absolute sector and lets the board work out where the
head has to be.

All communication is a sixteen byte Input Output Parameter Block
(IOPB) in main memory and one write-only attention port, at 0x90 and
0x91.  The host fills the block - the link to the next one, the
command, the drive, the sector, the count, and the address the data is
to move to or from - writes the attention port, and the board copies
the IOPB into its own memory, runs the command, moves the data with
its own dma channel, and writes the completion status back into the
block.  Writing 0xff to the port holds the 8085 in reset; writing
0x00 is the pulse that makes it fetch and run an IOPB.

The IOPB may begin anywhere in the 24 bit address space, and the first
fetch after a reset is from physical 0x50, which is not executed -
only the link bytes in it are kept.  So the driver brings the board up
by writing that link and pulsing it twice, after which each pulse
fetches the block the last one's link named.

The device number packs two things.  The major number is 8, and the
low two bits of the minor number name the drive.

.in +5
.nf
8/0  d3a        8/2  d3c
8/1  d3b        8/3  d3d
.in -5
.fi

Those four appear again 64 higher, as their whole-disk slices: 8/64 is
d3ac, the drive at cylinder 0 and unrotated, so that its block 0 is
physical block 0 and reading it reads the disk's label.

The geometry is not part of the name.  It comes off the disk, from the
label
.bd
mkfs
writes into the second half of the boot sector: a magic number, the
number of cylinders, heads and sectors, the rotation below, and the
size of the file system made on it.  A block number is a file system
block, and block 0 is not at cylinder 0, because the volume is rolled
so that the superblock and the inode list sit in the middle of the
platter rather than at its edge.  The driver reads both from the
label, so a volume keeps whatever rotation it was made with.

The commands the driver issues are few, and the board does the rest: a
global command the first time it uses the board, a specify per drive
as the drive is selected, a home to put a head at track 0, and then
reads and writes that carry nothing but the absolute sector, the count
and the address.  The board interrupts when a command has finished and
its status is in the IOPB, and the driver sleeps until it does, so a
seek is time the machine spends on something else.

A command that comes back an error is re-issued, and part way through
the driver re-homes the drive, since a head that has lost its place is
the usual reason a command fails.  A board that has not answered for a
second fails the request with NOTRDY rather than leaving a machine
that waits for ever; a board still silent on the next tick is a panic,
a DISK 3 that has stopped answering being a fault and not a slow disk.

The driver offers no raw command interface, so a disk on this
controller is not formatted through Micronix.  A drive must be
formatted elsewhere first, and then given a Micronix label by
.bd
mkfs
, which is what makes it a volume this system can mount.
.sp 2
.in -5
